d\": \"An enable pin on a switching regulator should be tied to VIN, or driven, so the converter starts deterministically. The spec describes U1 as the always-on 24 V to 5 V supply for the ESP32 and LDO.\",\n      \"evidence\": [\n        {\"source\": \"netlist\", \"quote\": \"(net (code \\\"136\\\") (name \\\"unconnected-(U1-EN-Pad2)\\\") (class \\\"Default\\\") (node (ref \\\"U1\\\") (pin \\\"2\\\") (pinfunction \\\"EN\\\") (pintype \\\"input+no_connect\\\")))\"},\n        {\"source\": \"spec\", \"quote\": \"U1 \u2014 TPS62933 (TI) | 24 V \u2192 5 V synchronous buck regulator, 3 A\"}\n      ],\n      \"impact\": \"If the EN pin does not have a guaranteed internal pull-up, the buck may fail to start or start unreliably, leaving the 5 V and 3.3 V rails and the ESP32 unpowered.\",\n      \"confidence\": 0.2\n    }\n  ],\n  \"summary\": \"The most concrete deviation is the RT timing resistor R13, whose tolerance metadata (\u00b120%) contradicts its \u00b11% part number and the rest of the design. A floating EN pin on the buck regulator U1 is a lower-confidence concern. The rest of the netlist shows no clear shorts or miswired nets.\"\n}",
